For each EOS, we then compare the inferred remnant mass to the "allowed" range between the maximum mass to avoid prompt collapse (using the relation M_{\\rm th}(R_{1.6},M_{\\rm max}) of Bauswein et al., 2013), to the minimum baryonic mass which results in a SMNS with an extractable energy \\Delta T (Eq. 1) less than the upper limits on the kinetic energy of the KN and GRB emission E_{\\rm EM}=E_{\\rm KN}+E_{\\rm GRB}\\lesssim 10^{51}\\,\\rm erg. Integrating the probability distribution of the remnant mass within this allowed range yields the "consistency" of the given EOS with the GW170817 observations,

{\\rm Consistency}=\\int_{S}P\\left(M_{\\rm rem}^{\\rm b}|\\mathcal{O},_{\\rm EoS} \\right)dM_{\\rm rem}^{\\rm b},  where S is the domain in which both \\Delta T(M_{\\rm rem}^{\\rm b})\\leq E_{\\rm EM} and M_{\\rm rem}^{\\rm b}\\leq M_{\\rm th}.

One example of this analysis is illustrated in Fig. 2. Clearly, E_{\\rm EM} is so much smaller than \\Delta T_{\\rm max} that the extractable energy curve intersects E_{\\rm EM} at the very precipice of the SMNS-HMNS transition. We also find for all the EOS we have examined that M_{\\rm smns}^{\\rm b}\\approx 1.18M_{\\rm max}^{\\rm b} largely irrespective of compactness, consistent with previous findings (e.g. Lasota et al., 1996). These two facts allow formulation of an approximate analytic criterion on the maximal non-rotating NS mass consistent with GW170817,

M_{\\rm max}^{\\rm b}\\lesssim M_{\\rm rem}^{\\rm b}/\\xi,  where \\xi\\simeq 1.16-1.21 and the EOS is only necessary in translating baryonic to gravitational masses.

